    "agent_messages": [
      "Refactoring the hand-written approval into a shared transition helper parameterized by operation/target status/response field, then registering both routes.\nI added `POST /purchases/bulk-reject`, but it has only been built, not run against a database. `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...` pass, and `go test ./...` passes only because the repo has no test files. Without the private database credentials, none of the transaction, idempotency or concurrency behaviour has been checked at runtime.\n\nThe only file changed is `internal/candidate/approval.go`. I turned the hand-written approve handler into one shared function, `decide(pool, op, toStatus, field)`, and registered both routes with it in `protocol.Routes`:\n- `/purchases/bulk-approve` → `bulk-approve.v3`, `approved`, `approvedIds`\n- `/purchases/bulk-reject` → `bulk-reject.v4`, `rejected`, `rejectedIds`\n\n**Bulk-approve stays the same.** Its SQL, lock key, request hash, status codes and response bytes are the same as before; the only difference is that the operation name, target status and response field are now passed in.\n\n**How reject behaves:**\n- **Same transaction as approve:** request body, identity, size limit and Idempotency-Key are checked by `protocol.HTTP`. The transaction then sets the guarded RLS context and takes an advisory lock on (operation, tenant, actor, key).\n- **Replays:** a replay of an earlier key either returns the saved response with `Idempotency-Replayed=true`, or 409 if the target set differs.\n- **All-or-nothing:** targets are locked with `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`. If any target is missing, in another tenant, created by the caller or not `submitted`, the whole batch gets 409 and nothing changes.\n- **Writes, all in one transaction:** the status change to `rejected`, one `ApprovalAudit` row per target (`toStatus='rejected'`, matching the existing \"one audit row per transition\" rule), and one `ActionRequest` row. That row has operation `bulk-reject.v4` and response `{\"rejectedIds\":[...]}`.\n- **Failures:** if any write fails, the whole transaction rolls back and the adapter returns 503, so the same key can be retried. The request hash includes the operation name, so the nightly ledger check still matches it.\n- **Concurrency and decided rows:** when two requests touch the same target, the row lock makes the second one see a status other than `submitted` and get 409. That covers approve vs reject and overlapping batches under different keys. Rejected rows can't be approved or rejected again, and approved rows can't be rejected, because both actions only update `status='submitted'`, in the SQL and in the RLS policy.\n\n`gofmt -l` flags `internal/protocol/protocol.go`. It was already like that, and that file is protected, so I left it alone."
    ],
